<Base title="about">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>I’m Deepak Nelapatla, a machine learning engineer on the ML4H team at the Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard.</p>
    <p>Most of my work is with longitudinal EHR notes: years of clinical text written about a patient over time. Right now I’m building models that use those notes to predict disease, focusing on late-life depression and stroke.</p>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>M.S. in Computer Science, concentration in AI/ML, University of Texas at Arlington</li>
      <li>B.Tech in Electronics and Communication Engineering, JNTU Hyderabad</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why “tokenizer”</h2>
    <p>Before a language model can read anything, a tokenizer breaks the text into small pieces. That’s what this site is for me: breaking work, life and play into pieces small enough to think about. On the homepage, a small model reads my system prompt and tries to predict what I’ll say, one token at a time. Where it guesses wrong, that’s me.</p>

    <h2>How notes grow</h2>
    <p>Most of these notes start with a pen in a paper notebook. Each one is marked with a stage:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Seedling</strong>: a rough idea, barely formed.</li>
      <li><strong>Budding</strong>: taking shape, still being revised.</li>
      <li><strong>Evergreen</strong>: finished enough to stand on its own.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <dl class="post-facts">
      <div><dt>Tokenizer</dt><dd>o200k_base (GPT-4o) via js-tiktoken, counted at build</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Funnel Display, Geist, Geist Mono</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Decode</dt><dd>Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, teacher-forced offline on my own words</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Astro, a few small inline scripts, no framework</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Hosted on</dt><dd>Vercel</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>Say hi</h2>
